@chapter 15
@title Cost-Volume-Profit (CVP) Analysis
@lo E1
@topic t1 | Breakeven and margin of safety | E1
@topic t2 | Changing price or costs | E1
@topic t3 | CVP charts | E1

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: How is contribution per unit calculated?
+ Selling price per unit less variable cost per unit || Contribution is what each unit contributes towards fixed costs and then profit.
- Selling price per unit less total cost per unit || Total cost per unit includes fixed cost, which gives profit per unit, not contribution.
- Selling price per unit less fixed cost per unit || Contribution deducts variable costs, not fixed costs.
- Total fixed costs divided by units sold || That is fixed cost per unit.
explain: Contribution per unit = selling price - variable cost per unit. Total contribution first covers fixed costs; anything left is profit.
mistake: Including fixed costs in contribution.
tip: Contribution ignores fixed costs.
src: Study text ch15 - contribution (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: At the breakeven point, which statement is true?
+ Total contribution equals total fixed costs || Profit is zero because contribution exactly covers fixed costs.
- Total revenue equals total variable costs || That would leave nothing to cover fixed costs, so there would be a loss.
- Contribution per unit equals fixed cost per unit at every output level || Fixed cost per unit changes with output.
- Total fixed costs are zero || Fixed costs still exist; they are just covered.
explain: Breakeven is where total revenue equals total costs, or equivalently where total contribution equals fixed costs.
mistake: Thinking breakeven means revenue covers variable costs.
tip: At breakeven: contribution = fixed costs; profit = 0.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product sells for $40 per unit and has variable costs of $24 per unit. Fixed costs are $96,000 per year. What is the breakeven point in units?
answer: 6000
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Breakeven point (units) = fixed costs / contribution per unit
step: Contribution per unit = 40 - 24 = $16.
step: Breakeven = 96,000 / 16 = 6,000 units.
check: 96000/(40-24)
explain: Each unit contributes $16 towards fixed costs, so 6,000 units are needed to cover $96,000.
mistake: Dividing by the selling price (2,400 units).
tip: Always divide fixed costs by CONTRIBUTION per unit.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What does the margin of safety measure?
+ How far sales can fall below budget before a loss is made || It is the gap between budgeted sales and breakeven sales.
- The profit earned at the budgeted level of sales || Related, but the margin of safety is a sales measure, not a profit figure.
- The difference between selling price and variable cost || That is contribution per unit.
- The level of sales needed to earn a target profit || That is the target-profit sales level.
explain: Margin of safety = budgeted sales - breakeven sales. It can be shown in units, revenue or as a percentage of budgeted sales.
mistake: Confusing margin of safety with profit or contribution.
tip: Margin of safety = cushion before loss.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product sells for $40 and has a variable cost of $24 per unit. What is its contribution to sales (C/S) ratio, as a percentage?
answer: 40
tol: 0
unit: %
formula: C/S ratio = contribution per unit / selling price per unit
step: Contribution = 40 - 24 = $16.
step: C/S ratio = 16 / 40 = 0.40 = 40%.
check: (40-24)/40*100
explain: The C/S ratio shows how much contribution each $1 of sales generates.
mistake: Dividing variable cost by selling price (60%).
tip: C/S ratio = contribution / sales.
src: Study text ch15 - C/S ratio (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: On a profit/volume (P/V) chart, where does the profit line cross the vertical (profit) axis?
+ At a loss equal to total fixed costs || At zero sales there is no contribution, so the loss equals fixed costs.
- At the breakeven point || Breakeven is where the line crosses the horizontal axis.
- At zero || At zero sales the business makes a loss.
- At the maximum profit || The vertical-axis point is the zero-sales position.
explain: A P/V chart plots profit against sales. It starts at minus fixed costs and rises at the rate of contribution; breakeven is where it crosses the sales axis.
mistake: Reading breakeven from the vertical axis.
tip: Vertical axis = fixed costs (as a loss); horizontal axis crossing = breakeven.
src: Study text ch15 - P/V chart (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: If a company raises its fixed costs and all other factors stay the same, what happens to the breakeven point?
+ It rises || More contribution, and therefore more units, is needed to cover the higher fixed costs.
- It falls || Higher fixed costs need more units to cover them.
- It stays the same || Breakeven depends on fixed costs.
- It becomes zero || Breakeven exists while there are fixed costs.
explain: Breakeven = fixed costs / contribution per unit. A higher numerator gives a higher breakeven point.
mistake: Thinking fixed costs do not affect breakeven because they do not change with volume.
tip: Use the formula to reason about every change.
src: Study text ch15 - changing costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: match
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Match each CVP measure with its formula.
pair: Breakeven point in units => Fixed costs / contribution per unit
pair: C/S ratio => Contribution per unit / selling price per unit
pair: Breakeven revenue => Fixed costs / C/S ratio
pair: Margin of safety => Budgeted sales - breakeven sales
choice: Fixed costs / selling price per unit
explain: These four formulas underpin most CVP questions. Dividing fixed costs by selling price ignores variable costs and is a common error.
mistake: Choosing "fixed costs / selling price" for breakeven units.
tip: Learn the formulas as a set.
src: Study text ch15 - CVP formulas (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Budgeted sales are 8,000 units and the breakeven point is 6,000 units. What is the margin of safety in units?
answer: 2000
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Margin of safety = budgeted sales - breakeven sales
step: Budgeted sales = 8,000 units; breakeven = 6,000 units.
step: 8,000 - 6,000 = 2,000 units.
check: 8000-6000
explain: Sales could fall by 2,000 units before the business makes a loss.
mistake: Dividing instead of subtracting when units are asked for.
tip: Read whether the answer is wanted in units, revenue or %.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which assumption underlies basic CVP analysis?
+ Fixed costs and variable cost per unit stay constant over the relevant range || Linear cost behaviour within the relevant range is assumed.
- Selling price falls as more units are sold || Basic CVP assumes a constant selling price.
- All costs are variable || CVP separates fixed and variable costs.
- Inventory levels change every period || CVP assumes production equals sales.
explain: CVP assumes a constant selling price, constant variable cost per unit, constant total fixed costs within the relevant range, and that production equals sales.
mistake: Forgetting that the assumptions limit how far CVP results can be trusted.
tip: Assumptions are often tested as "which is NOT an assumption".
src: Study text ch15 - CVP assumptions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product sells for $40 with a variable cost of $24 per unit. Fixed costs are $96,000. How many units must be sold to earn a profit of $32,000?
answer: 8000
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Units for target profit = (fixed costs + target profit) / contribution per unit
step: Contribution per unit = 40 - 24 = $16.
step: Required contribution = 96,000 + 32,000 = $128,000.
step: Units = 128,000 / 16 = 8,000.
check: (96000+32000)/16
explain: Contribution must cover fixed costs first and then provide the target profit.
mistake: Forgetting to add the target profit to fixed costs.
tip: Target profit questions = breakeven formula with the target added on top.
src: Study text ch15 - target profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q012
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Fixed costs are $96,000 and the C/S ratio is 40%. What is the breakeven revenue?
+ $240,000 || 96,000 / 0.40 = $240,000.
- $38,400 || This multiplies fixed costs by the ratio.
- $134,400 || This adds 40% to fixed costs.
- $160,000 || This divides by 60%, the variable cost ratio.
formula: Breakeven revenue = fixed costs / C/S ratio
step: 96,000 / 0.40 = $240,000.
step: Check: 6,000 units x $40 = $240,000.
check: 96000/0.4
explain: The C/S ratio method gives breakeven in revenue directly, which is useful when unit data are not given.
mistake: Multiplying instead of dividing.
tip: Breakeven revenue = fixed costs / C/S ratio.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven revenue (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Budgeted sales are 8,000 units and breakeven is 6,000 units. What is the margin of safety as a percentage of budgeted sales?
answer: 25
tol: 0
unit: %
formula: Margin of safety % = (budgeted sales - breakeven sales) / budgeted sales x 100
step: Margin of safety = 8,000 - 6,000 = 2,000 units.
step: 2,000 / 8,000 x 100 = 25%.
check: (8000-6000)/8000*100
explain: The percentage is always calculated on BUDGETED sales.
mistake: Dividing by breakeven sales (33.3%).
tip: Denominator = budgeted sales.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A product currently sells for $40 with a variable cost of $24 and fixed costs of $96,000. If the price is cut to $36, what is the new breakeven point?
+ 8,000 units || New contribution = 36 - 24 = 12; 96,000 / 12 = 8,000.
- 6,000 units || This is the old breakeven point.
- 2,667 units || This divides fixed costs by the new selling price ($36) instead of by contribution.
- 4,000 units || This divides by a contribution of $24.
formula: Breakeven = fixed costs / new contribution per unit
step: New contribution = 36 - 24 = $12.
step: Breakeven = 96,000 / 12 = 8,000 units.
check: 96000/(36-24)
explain: A price cut reduces contribution per unit, so more units are needed to break even.
mistake: Assuming a lower price automatically improves the result.
tip: Recalculate contribution before recalculating breakeven.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Contribution is $16 per unit and fixed costs are $96,000. Fixed costs rise by $8,000. What is the new breakeven point (units)?
answer: 6500
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Breakeven = fixed costs / contribution per unit
step: New fixed costs = 96,000 + 8,000 = $104,000.
step: Breakeven = 104,000 / 16 = 6,500 units.
check: (96000+8000)/16
explain: Each extra $16 of fixed cost requires one more unit to be sold to break even.
mistake: Dividing only the increase by contribution (500 units) and stopping.
tip: Increase in breakeven = increase in fixed costs / contribution per unit = 500 units.
src: Study text ch15 - changing costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product has contribution of $16 per unit and fixed costs of $96,000. What profit is earned if 9,000 units are sold?
+ $48,000 || 9,000 x 16 - 96,000 = $48,000.
- $144,000 || This is total contribution before fixed costs.
- $96,000 || This is the fixed cost figure.
- $32,000 || This is the profit at 8,000 units.
formula: Profit = units x contribution per unit - fixed costs
step: Contribution = 9,000 x 16 = $144,000.
step: Profit = 144,000 - 96,000 = $48,000.
check: 9000*16 - 96000
explain: Profit is total contribution less fixed costs.
mistake: Reporting contribution as profit.
tip: Contribution - fixed costs = profit.
src: Study text ch15 - profit calculation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: On a traditional breakeven chart, where is the breakeven point?
+ Where the sales revenue line crosses the total cost line || Revenue equals total cost there.
- Where the fixed cost line crosses the vertical axis || That shows the level of fixed costs.
- Where the variable cost line crosses the sales line || The variable cost line is not normally drawn separately on a traditional chart.
- At the highest point of the sales line || The highest point is the end of the volume range.
explain: A breakeven chart shows fixed costs, total costs and sales revenue against volume. Breakeven is where revenue equals total cost.
mistake: Reading breakeven from the fixed cost line.
tip: Breakeven chart - revenue meets total cost; P/V chart - profit line meets the axis.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven charts (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Variable cost per unit increases while selling price and fixed costs stay the same. What is the effect?
+ Contribution per unit falls and the breakeven point rises || Higher variable cost reduces contribution, so more units are needed.
- Contribution per unit rises and the breakeven point falls || Higher variable cost reduces contribution.
- The breakeven point is unchanged because fixed costs are unchanged || Breakeven depends on contribution as well.
- Only the margin of safety rises || The margin of safety falls as breakeven rises.
explain: Breakeven = fixed costs / contribution per unit. Lower contribution means a higher breakeven point and a lower margin of safety.
mistake: Thinking only fixed costs affect breakeven.
tip: Trace each change through contribution, then breakeven, then margin of safety.
src: Study text ch15 - changing costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Fixed costs are $96,000, selling price $40 and variable cost $26 per unit. How many WHOLE units must be sold to at least break even?
answer: 6858
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Breakeven = fixed costs / contribution per unit, rounded UP to whole units
step: Contribution = 40 - 26 = $14.
step: 96,000 / 14 = 6,857.14 units.
step: Selling 6,857 units leaves a small loss, so 6,858 whole units are needed.
check: -(-96000//14)
explain: When the breakeven calculation gives a fraction of a unit, round up to the next whole unit to avoid a loss.
mistake: Rounding down to 6,857 units.
tip: Breakeven in whole units always rounds UP.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Sales revenue $500,000; variable costs $300,000; fixed costs $120,000.
q: What is the breakeven revenue?
+ $300,000 || C/S ratio = 200,000 / 500,000 = 40%; 120,000 / 0.40 = $300,000.
- $200,000 || This is total contribution.
- $420,000 || This is total cost.
- $120,000 || This is the fixed cost figure, not breakeven revenue.
formula: Breakeven revenue = fixed costs / C/S ratio
step: Contribution = 500,000 - 300,000 = $200,000.
step: C/S ratio = 200,000 / 500,000 = 0.40.
step: Breakeven revenue = 120,000 / 0.40 = $300,000.
check: 120000/((500000-300000)/500000)
explain: The C/S ratio can be found from total figures when unit data are not given.
mistake: Confusing breakeven revenue with total cost.
tip: When only totals are given, use the C/S ratio method.
src: Study text ch15 - C/S ratio method (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Sales revenue $500,000; variable costs $300,000; fixed costs $120,000. Breakeven revenue is $300,000.
q: What is the margin of safety as a percentage of sales?
+ 40% || (500,000 - 300,000) / 500,000 = 40%.
- 66.7% || This divides by breakeven revenue.
- 60% || This is breakeven revenue as a percentage of sales.
- 16% || This is profit as a percentage of sales.
formula: Margin of safety % = (sales - breakeven sales) / sales x 100
step: Margin of safety = 500,000 - 300,000 = $200,000.
step: 200,000 / 500,000 = 40%.
check: (500000-300000)/500000*100
explain: Sales could fall by 40% before the business makes a loss.
mistake: Dividing by breakeven revenue.
tip: Margin of safety % uses budgeted (or actual) sales as the denominator.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What is the main advantage of a contribution breakeven chart over a traditional breakeven chart?
+ Contribution can be seen directly, because the variable cost line is drawn first with fixed costs above it || The gap between sales and variable costs is visible.
- It shows the breakeven point more accurately || Both charts give the same breakeven point.
- It does not require fixed costs to be known || Fixed costs are still needed.
- It shows profit at every level on the vertical axis || That is the P/V chart.
explain: In a contribution chart the variable cost line starts from the origin and fixed costs are added above it, so contribution at any volume can be read off.
mistake: Thinking different charts give different breakeven points.
tip: All CVP charts show the same breakeven; they differ in what else is visible.
src: Study text ch15 - CVP charts (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Fixed costs are $60,000, target profit is $30,000 and the C/S ratio is 30%. What sales revenue ($) is needed to achieve the target profit?
answer: 300000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revenue for target profit = (fixed costs + target profit) / C/S ratio
step: Required contribution = 60,000 + 30,000 = $90,000.
step: Revenue = 90,000 / 0.30 = $300,000.
check: (60000+30000)/0.3
explain: Each $1 of sales gives $0.30 of contribution; $90,000 of contribution therefore needs $300,000 of sales.
mistake: Forgetting to add the target profit.
tip: Target profit in revenue uses the C/S ratio in place of contribution per unit.
src: Study text ch15 - target profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: The breakeven point is 5,000 units and contribution is $12 per unit. What are total fixed costs?
+ $60,000 || At breakeven, contribution = fixed costs: 5,000 x 12.
- $417 || This divides 5,000 by 12.
- $5,012 || This adds the two figures.
- $120,000 || This doubles the correct answer.
formula: Fixed costs = breakeven units x contribution per unit
step: At breakeven, total contribution = fixed costs.
step: 5,000 x 12 = $60,000.
check: 5000*12
explain: Rearranging the breakeven formula gives fixed costs.
mistake: Dividing instead of multiplying.
tip: At breakeven: units x contribution = fixed costs.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO changes would REDUCE a product's breakeven point (all other factors unchanged)?
+ An increase in selling price || Contribution per unit rises.
+ A decrease in fixed costs || Less contribution is needed to break even.
- An increase in variable cost per unit || Contribution falls and breakeven rises.
- An increase in budgeted sales volume || Budgeted volume affects the margin of safety, not breakeven.
explain: Breakeven falls when contribution per unit rises (higher price or lower variable cost) or when fixed costs fall.
mistake: Thinking higher budgeted sales lower breakeven.
tip: Budgeted sales change the margin of safety, not breakeven.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price or costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Fixed costs are $50,000, variable cost is $15 per unit and the breakeven point is 2,500 units. What is the selling price per unit ($)?
answer: 35
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Contribution per unit = fixed costs / breakeven units; price = contribution + variable cost
step: Contribution per unit = 50,000 / 2,500 = $20.
step: Price = 20 + 15 = $35.
check: 50000/2500 + 15
explain: Working backwards from breakeven gives the contribution per unit and therefore the selling price.
mistake: Stopping at the contribution of $20.
tip: Price = contribution + variable cost.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which of the following is NOT an assumption of basic CVP analysis?
+ Fixed costs change in proportion to sales volume || CVP assumes fixed costs are constant within the relevant range.
- The selling price per unit is constant || This is a CVP assumption.
- The variable cost per unit is constant || This is a CVP assumption.
- Production volume equals sales volume || This is a CVP assumption.
explain: CVP assumes linear revenue and cost behaviour, constant fixed costs and no change in inventory.
mistake: Choosing a genuine assumption because it seems unrealistic.
tip: "NOT an assumption" - find the statement that contradicts CVP.
src: Study text ch15 - assumptions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A business sells 8,000 units at $40 with a variable cost of $24 and fixed costs of $96,000. It wants a profit of $40,000 without changing volume or costs. What selling price is needed?
+ $41 || Required contribution per unit = (96,000 + 40,000) / 8,000 = $17; price = 17 + 24 = $41.
- $17 || This is the required contribution per unit, not the price.
- $57 || This adds the required contribution to $40 instead of to variable cost.
- $42 || This rounds the required contribution up to $18.
formula: Required contribution per unit = (fixed costs + target profit) / units; price = contribution + variable cost
step: Required contribution = 96,000 + 40,000 = $136,000.
step: Per unit = 136,000 / 8,000 = $17.
step: Price = 17 + 24 = $41.
check: (96000+40000)/8000 + 24
explain: When volume is fixed, the target profit determines the contribution needed per unit, and therefore the price.
mistake: Giving the contribution per unit as the price.
tip: Finish the calculation - add variable cost back.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: On a P/V chart, what does the slope of the profit line represent?
+ The contribution earned per unit (or per $ of sales) || Each extra unit adds its contribution to profit.
- The fixed cost per unit || Fixed costs determine where the line starts, not its slope.
- The selling price per unit || The slope reflects contribution, which is price less variable cost.
- The margin of safety || The margin of safety is a distance along the sales axis.
explain: The P/V line starts at minus fixed costs and rises by the contribution of each unit sold. A steeper line means a higher contribution per unit.
mistake: Linking the slope to selling price.
tip: Intercept = fixed costs; slope = contribution.
src: Study text ch15 - P/V chart (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each change with its effect on the breakeven point (all other factors unchanged).
pair: Selling price rises => Breakeven falls
pair: Fixed costs rise => Breakeven rises
pair: Variable cost per unit falls => Breakeven falls
pair: Budgeted sales volume rises => Breakeven unchanged
explain: Breakeven = fixed costs / (price - variable cost). Budgeted volume does not appear in the formula.
mistake: Thinking higher budgeted sales lower breakeven.
tip: Check whether the changed item is in the formula.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price or costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Budgeted sales are 8,000 units, breakeven is 6,000 units and contribution is $16 per unit. What is the budgeted profit?
+ $32,000 || Margin of safety 2,000 units x $16 = $32,000.
- $128,000 || This is total contribution at budget.
- $96,000 || This is total fixed costs.
- $16,000 || This uses 1,000 units.
formula: Profit = margin of safety (units) x contribution per unit
step: Margin of safety = 8,000 - 6,000 = 2,000 units.
step: Profit = 2,000 x 16 = $32,000.
check: (8000-6000)*16
explain: Sales up to breakeven cover fixed costs; every unit above breakeven adds its full contribution to profit.
mistake: Reporting total contribution as profit.
tip: Profit = margin of safety units x contribution per unit.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety and profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Fixed costs do not change. Which decision rule maximises total profit?
+ Choose the option that maximises total contribution || With fixed costs unchanged, extra contribution is extra profit.
- Choose the option with the highest selling price || A high price may reduce volume and total contribution.
- Choose the option with the highest contribution per unit || A higher per-unit contribution can still give a lower total if volume falls.
- Choose the option with the lowest breakeven point || A low breakeven does not guarantee the highest profit.
explain: When fixed costs are unaffected by the choice, profit differences come only from total contribution.
mistake: Focusing on per-unit figures instead of totals.
tip: Compare TOTAL contribution across options.
src: Study text ch15 - decisions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q033
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company sells 10,000 units with contribution of $8 per unit. It expects a 10% increase in sales volume with no change in price, variable cost per unit or fixed costs. By how much ($) will profit increase?
answer: 8000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Change in profit = change in units x contribution per unit (fixed costs unchanged)
step: Extra units = 10% x 10,000 = 1,000.
step: Extra profit = 1,000 x 8 = $8,000.
check: 0.1*10000*8
explain: Because fixed costs do not change, each extra unit adds its full contribution to profit.
mistake: Applying 10% to profit instead of to contribution.
tip: Volume changes affect profit through contribution only.
src: Study text ch15 - CVP and volume (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product's C/S ratio is 25% and fixed costs are $45,000. Budgeted sales revenue is $240,000. What is the margin of safety in revenue?
+ $60,000 || Breakeven revenue = 45,000 / 0.25 = 180,000; 240,000 - 180,000 = $60,000.
- $180,000 || This is breakeven revenue.
- $15,000 || This deducts fixed costs from budgeted contribution - which is profit.
- $195,000 || This deducts fixed costs from budgeted sales.
formula: Margin of safety = budgeted revenue - breakeven revenue
step: Breakeven revenue = 45,000 / 0.25 = $180,000.
step: Margin of safety = 240,000 - 180,000 = $60,000.
check: 240000 - 45000/0.25
explain: The margin of safety in revenue is the gap between budgeted and breakeven sales value.
mistake: Giving the breakeven revenue as the answer.
tip: Two steps: breakeven first, then the margin.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: Which formula gives the number of units needed to earn a target profit?
+ (Fixed costs + target profit) / contribution per unit || Contribution must cover fixed costs and then the profit.
- Fixed costs / contribution per unit + target profit || The target profit must be divided by contribution too.
- (Fixed costs + target profit) / selling price per unit || Divide by contribution, not price.
- (Fixed costs - target profit) / contribution per unit || The target profit is added.
explain: The target profit formula extends the breakeven formula by adding the target to fixed costs.
mistake: Adding the target profit after dividing.
tip: Put the target profit inside the brackets.
src: Study text ch15 - target profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc, trap
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A product sells 8,000 units at $40 each, with a variable cost of $24 per unit and fixed costs of $96,000. Marketing proposes cutting the price to $36, which it expects to raise sales to 10,000 units.
q: What is the effect of the proposal on profit?
+ Profit falls by $8,000 || Current profit 8,000 x 16 - 96,000 = 32,000; new profit 10,000 x 12 - 96,000 = 24,000.
- Profit rises by $8,000 || Higher volume does not make up for the lower contribution per unit.
- Profit rises by $32,000 || This multiplies the extra 2,000 units by the old contribution of $16.
- Profit is unchanged || Total contribution changes from 128,000 to 120,000.
formula: Profit = units x contribution per unit - fixed costs
step: Current: 8,000 x (40 - 24) - 96,000 = $32,000.
step: Proposed: 10,000 x (36 - 24) - 96,000 = $24,000.
step: Change = 24,000 - 32,000 = -$8,000.
check: (8000*16 - 96000) - (10000*12 - 96000)
explain: A price cut reduces contribution on EVERY unit, not just the extra units. Total contribution falls unless volume rises enough.
mistake: Valuing only the extra units at the old contribution.
tip: Compare total contribution before and after.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q037
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A product sells 8,000 units at $40 each, with a variable cost of $24 per unit and fixed costs of $96,000. The price is to be cut to $36.
q: How many units must be sold at the new price to keep profit at its current level?
answer: 10667
tol: 0
unit: units
formula: Units = (fixed costs + current profit) / new contribution per unit, rounded up
step: Current profit = 8,000 x 16 - 96,000 = $32,000.
step: New contribution = 36 - 24 = $12.
step: Units = (96,000 + 32,000) / 12 = 10,666.67, so 10,667 whole units.
check: -(-(96000+32000)//12)
explain: Keeping profit constant after a price cut requires a large rise in volume (here about 33%).
mistake: Using the old contribution of $16.
tip: The current profit becomes the target profit.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc, mixed
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: Current: price $50, variable cost $30, fixed costs $120,000, sales 9,000 units. Proposal: buy a machine that raises fixed costs by $30,000 but reduces variable cost to $25 per unit.
q: What is the new breakeven point, and what happens to profit at 9,000 units?
+ 6,000 units; profit rises by $15,000 || New contribution 25; breakeven 150,000 / 25 = 6,000. Profit: 9,000 x 25 - 150,000 = 75,000 vs 9,000 x 20 - 120,000 = 60,000.
- 6,000 units; profit falls by $15,000 || Profit rises because the extra contribution (45,000) exceeds the extra fixed cost (30,000).
- 7,500 units; profit rises by $15,000 || 7,500 is the breakeven if fixed costs rose but variable cost did not fall.
- 4,800 units; profit rises by $45,000 || This ignores the extra fixed costs.
formula: Breakeven = fixed costs / contribution per unit; profit = units x contribution - fixed costs
step: Old: contribution 20; breakeven 120,000 / 20 = 6,000; profit 9,000 x 20 - 120,000 = 60,000.
step: New: contribution 25; breakeven 150,000 / 25 = 6,000; profit 9,000 x 25 - 150,000 = 75,000.
step: Breakeven unchanged at 6,000 units; profit up $15,000.
check: 150000/25
explain: Changes in fixed and variable costs can offset each other in the breakeven point while still changing profit at a given volume.
mistake: Judging the proposal by breakeven alone.
tip: Check both breakeven (risk) and profit (return).
src: Study text ch15 - changing costs (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company's profit is $36,000 when sales are $360,000, and $60,000 when sales are $420,000. Price and costs behave linearly. What is the breakeven revenue?
+ $270,000 || C/S ratio = 24,000 / 60,000 = 40%; fixed costs = 0.4 x 360,000 - 36,000 = 108,000; 108,000 / 0.4 = 270,000.
- $108,000 || This is the fixed cost figure, not breakeven revenue.
- $324,000 || This deducts the first profit from sales.
- $300,000 || This uses a C/S ratio of 36%.
formula: C/S ratio = change in profit / change in sales; breakeven revenue = fixed costs / C/S ratio
step: Change in profit = 24,000; change in sales = 60,000; C/S ratio = 40% (fixed costs do not change).
step: Fixed costs = contribution - profit = 0.4 x 360,000 - 36,000 = $108,000.
step: Breakeven revenue = 108,000 / 0.4 = $270,000.
check: (0.4*360000 - 36000)/0.4
explain: Because fixed costs are constant, the change in profit equals the change in contribution, which gives the C/S ratio.
mistake: Dividing profit by sales to find a "C/S ratio".
tip: Two levels of activity - use the change method (like high-low).
src: Study text ch15 - C/S ratio (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A student reads a P/V chart and says: "The line starts at -$80,000 on the profit axis, so the business breaks even at sales of $80,000." What is wrong with this statement?
+ -$80,000 is the loss at zero sales (the fixed costs); breakeven is where the line crosses the sales axis || The vertical intercept shows fixed costs, not breakeven sales.
- Nothing - breakeven sales always equal fixed costs || Breakeven sales equal fixed costs divided by the C/S ratio.
- The line should start at zero || A P/V line starts at minus fixed costs.
- Breakeven is where the line is steepest || The P/V line is straight, with a constant slope.
explain: On a P/V chart, the vertical intercept is minus fixed costs; breakeven is the sales level where profit is zero.
mistake: Equating breakeven revenue with fixed costs.
tip: Breakeven revenue is ALWAYS larger than fixed costs (unless the C/S ratio is 100%).
src: Study text ch15 - P/V chart (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: A product has a selling price of $25 and a C/S ratio of 32%. Fixed costs are $64,000 and budgeted sales are 10,000 units. What is the margin of safety as a percentage of budgeted sales?
answer: 20
tol: 0
unit: %
formula: Breakeven units = fixed costs / contribution per unit; margin of safety % = (budget - breakeven) / budget x 100
step: Contribution per unit = 32% x 25 = $8.
step: Breakeven = 64,000 / 8 = 8,000 units.
step: Margin of safety = (10,000 - 8,000) / 10,000 = 20%.
check: (10000 - 64000/(0.32*25))/10000*100
explain: The C/S ratio converts price into contribution per unit, which then gives breakeven in units.
mistake: Dividing fixed costs by the C/S ratio and comparing revenue with units.
tip: Keep units with units and revenue with revenue.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements about the margin of safety are correct?
+ A high margin of safety indicates a low risk of making a loss || Sales could fall a long way before a loss.
+ Profit equals the margin of safety in units multiplied by contribution per unit || All fixed costs are covered at breakeven, so units above it add pure contribution.
- The margin of safety rises when fixed costs rise || Higher fixed costs raise breakeven and reduce the margin.
- The margin of safety is measured from actual production, not sales || It is based on sales.
explain: The margin of safety measures the cushion between expected sales and breakeven sales, and links directly to profit.
mistake: Thinking higher fixed costs give more safety.
tip: Margin of safety and profit move together.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A product has contribution of $15 per unit and fixed costs of $90,000. Budgeted sales are 8,000 units. Management is considering advertising costing $12,000, which would raise sales to 9,000 units. Should it go ahead?
+ Yes - profit rises by $3,000, because the extra contribution of $15,000 exceeds the $12,000 cost || 1,000 extra units x 15 = 15,000 > 12,000.
- No - breakeven rises from 6,000 to 6,800 units || Breakeven does rise, but profit still increases.
- No - profit falls by $12,000 || This ignores the extra contribution.
- Yes - profit rises by $15,000 || This ignores the advertising cost.
formula: Change in profit = extra contribution - extra fixed costs
step: Extra contribution = 1,000 x 15 = $15,000.
step: Change in profit = 15,000 - 12,000 = +$3,000.
check: 1000*15 - 12000
explain: Incremental analysis compares the extra contribution with the extra fixed cost.
mistake: Rejecting the proposal because breakeven rises.
tip: Decisions are judged on profit; breakeven indicates risk.
src: Study text ch15 - decisions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each CVP chart feature with what it shows.
pair: Vertical intercept of a P/V chart => Fixed costs (as a loss)
pair: Gap between sales and total cost lines beyond breakeven => Profit
pair: Gap between sales and variable cost lines on a contribution chart => Contribution
pair: Horizontal distance between budgeted sales and breakeven => Margin of safety
choice: Selling price per unit
explain: Each chart highlights different measures. Reading the right gap or intercept is a common exam skill.
mistake: Mixing up profit and contribution gaps.
tip: Contribution gap uses VARIABLE cost; profit gap uses TOTAL cost.
src: Study text ch15 - CVP charts (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company cuts its selling price by 10%. Its C/S ratio was 40% before the cut and variable cost per unit is unchanged. What is the new C/S ratio?
+ 33.3% || Price 100 -> 90; variable cost 60; contribution 30; 30 / 90 = 33.3%.
- 30% || This deducts 10 percentage points from 40%.
- 36% || This reduces the ratio by 10% of itself.
- 40% || The ratio changes because price changes but variable cost does not.
formula: C/S ratio = (price - variable cost) / price
step: Assume old price $100: variable cost = $60, contribution = $40.
step: New price = $90; contribution = 90 - 60 = $30.
step: New C/S ratio = 30 / 90 = 33.3%.
check: (90-60)/90*100
explain: Using an illustrative price of $100 makes percentage changes easy to work through.
mistake: Subtracting percentage points directly.
tip: Use $100 as a base price for percentage questions.
src: Study text ch15 - changing price (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: At sales of 12,000 units a company makes a profit of $30,000. Its breakeven point is 9,000 units. What are total fixed costs ($)?
answer: 90000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Contribution per unit = profit / margin of safety units; fixed costs = breakeven units x contribution per unit
step: Margin of safety = 12,000 - 9,000 = 3,000 units, earning the $30,000 profit.
step: Contribution per unit = 30,000 / 3,000 = $10.
step: Fixed costs = 9,000 x 10 = $90,000.
check: 9000*30000/(12000-9000)
explain: Profit is earned only on units above breakeven, which reveals contribution per unit.
mistake: Dividing profit by total units (12,000).
tip: Profit / margin of safety units = contribution per unit.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: Product A has a breakeven point of 4,000 units and budgeted sales of 5,000 units. Product B has a breakeven point of 6,000 units and budgeted sales of 9,000 units. Which statement is correct?
+ Product B is less risky, with a margin of safety of 33.3% against 20% for A || A: 1,000 / 5,000 = 20%; B: 3,000 / 9,000 = 33.3%.
- Product A is less risky because its breakeven point is lower || Risk depends on the margin of safety, not breakeven alone.
- They are equally risky because both make a profit || Their margins of safety differ.
- Product B is more risky because its breakeven point is higher || A higher breakeven point can still be well below budgeted sales.
formula: Margin of safety % = (budget - breakeven) / budget x 100
step: A: (5,000 - 4,000) / 5,000 = 20%.
step: B: (9,000 - 6,000) / 9,000 = 33.3%.
check: (9000-6000)/9000*100
explain: The margin of safety percentage compares products of different sizes on a like-for-like basis.
mistake: Comparing breakeven points alone.
tip: Risk = how far sales can fall in percentage terms.
src: Study text ch15 - margin of safety (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: Fixed costs are $120,000. The selling price is $60 and variable cost is $36 per unit. Fixed costs are expected to rise by 10% and variable cost per unit by $4. What is the new breakeven point?
+ 6,600 units || Fixed costs 132,000; contribution 60 - 40 = 20; 132,000 / 20 = 6,600.
- 5,500 units || This includes the fixed cost rise but not the variable cost rise (132,000 / 24).
- 6,000 units || This includes the variable cost rise but not the fixed cost rise (120,000 / 20).
- 5,000 units || This is the original breakeven point.
formula: Breakeven = new fixed costs / new contribution per unit
step: New fixed costs = 120,000 x 1.10 = $132,000.
step: New contribution = 60 - (36 + 4) = $20.
step: Breakeven = 132,000 / 20 = 6,600 units.
check: 120000*1.1/(60-40)
explain: When several factors change at once, recalculate each part of the formula before dividing.
mistake: Applying only one of the two changes.
tip: Update numerator and denominator separately.
src: Study text ch15 - changing costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company makes several products in a fixed sales mix. A manager calculates a single breakeven point using the average C/S ratio. Which limitation applies?
+ The breakeven point is valid only if the sales mix stays as assumed || A change in mix changes the average C/S ratio and the breakeven point.
- A single breakeven point can never be calculated for more than one product || It can be, using the weighted average C/S ratio.
- The average C/S ratio ignores fixed costs || Fixed costs are divided by the ratio; they are not ignored.
- The breakeven point is always understated || It may be under- or overstated depending on how the mix changes.
explain: Multi-product CVP relies on a constant mix. If sales shift towards low-contribution products, the real breakeven point is higher.
mistake: Treating a multi-product breakeven as fixed regardless of mix.
tip: Constant sales mix is an extra assumption when there is more than one product.
src: Study text ch15 - CVP limitations (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t1
status: review
important: 1
q: How is the breakeven point in sales revenue calculated?
+ Fixed costs / C/S ratio || Each $1 of sales covers C/S ratio dollars of fixed cost.
- Fixed costs x C/S ratio || Multiplying gives a smaller figure than fixed costs.
- Fixed costs / selling price || This gives units, and ignores variable costs.
- Contribution / fixed costs || This is not a breakeven measure.
explain: Breakeven revenue = fixed costs / C/S ratio, or breakeven units x selling price.
mistake: Multiplying fixed costs by the ratio.
tip: Check: breakeven revenue must be more than fixed costs.
src: Study text ch15 - breakeven revenue (concept basis; original question)
